WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:03.670
Film Courage: 5 things an actor should not
do during an audition?

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:08.870
Aimee Garcia, Actress: Ai yai yai…I think
everything is on the table.

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:13.680
George Lopez, Actor/Comedian: Tell them how
funny the material is or how well it’s written.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.309
That’s insulting because “You know?

00:00:15.309 --> 00:00:16.309
This is really funny.”

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:18.640
Well…how did you think it was going to be?

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.080
So that’s one I would say.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.080
Aimee: Oops.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:25.119
George: Don’t ask them to validate your
parking.

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:31.340
Don’t say “Usually when they put me on
tape, I’m not wearing anything.”

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.670
Film Courage: Okay?

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:34.670
Unless you’re in The Valley.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:35.670
George: You guys got two?

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.530
Raúl Castillo, Actor: You’ve got one?

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:38.539
I’ve got one.

00:00:38.539 --> 00:00:39.679
Aimee: Alright, go for it.

00:00:39.679 --> 00:00:45.350
Raúl: I think one thing you shouldn’t do
is you shouldn’t shake hands with anyone.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:49.260
You shouldn’t presume that someone is going
to greet you openly.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.480
Aimee: Wow…well I have failed all of these.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.440
I’m like “This is so funny!”

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.420
And I give them a hug…so fail, fail.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.519
George: Fail, fail, fail.

00:00:59.519 --> 00:01:02.139
Go ahead, there’s one left.

00:01:02.139 --> 00:01:06.210
Aimee: Let’s see…for me it’s just don’t
overthink.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.470
Just go.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:12.250
I just say “Hey this is my gift to you,
have fun.”

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:13.800
Instead of like “Is this what you want?”

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:14.800
I never think that.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.980
I think “Hi, this is my gift.”

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:17.980
Microphone drop and…

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:20.470
George: Okay, so that’s why you don’t
work more.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:21.990
George Lopez.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:27.500
Film Courage: Okay so once you get the [acting]
job, what are five things an actor does on

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.700
set that are huge mistakes?

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.860
Aimee: Direct another actor.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:38.480
Raúl: They were trying to direct me left
and right.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.730
George: Oh no…listen…no way.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:42.300
I think sit in somebody’s chair.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.300
Aimee: Really?

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.430
George: Yes, if you sit an executive director’s
chair as an actor.

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:51.090
Just go to catering and shut up.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:58.680
Aimee: What bothers me is you have a 200-person
crew, people wake up at 4:00 in the morning,

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:04.710
they are spending 17 hours a day and I really
don’t like when people aren’t prepared.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:05.870
That’s my big thing.

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:07.030
To me acting is like tennis.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.420
You can’t out act somebody.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:09.569
You throw them the ball.

00:02:09.569 --> 00:02:13.139
Depending on how they hit the ball to you
is how you hit it back.

00:02:13.139 --> 00:02:16.630
To me it’s just be really prepared.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:21.930
I think even if that means whatever like not
knowing the lines at all but you kind of know

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:23.310
what the scene is about.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:25.469
Whatever…you know the lines inside out.

00:02:25.469 --> 00:02:26.469
Whatever it is.

00:02:26.469 --> 00:02:32.730
I just feel it’s really disrespectful if
people are figuring it out there while everyone

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.020
is waiting.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.090
Right?

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.400
I think that’s like the worst thing.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.400
Raúl: I think that’s right on.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.730
George: Leave before someone is coverage,
like you go somewhere and you’re not there

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.209
for the other actor.

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:46.040
You have to go to dinner or something.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:51.189
Aimee: They’ll be like “Act with this
piece of tape.”

00:02:51.189 --> 00:02:58.029
George: Or talk loud about something that
nobody cares about which would be their own

00:02:58.029 --> 00:03:00.269
life.

00:03:00.269 --> 00:03:05.927
Film Courage: For this film did Ben Hernandez
Bray [co-writer/director of EL CHICANO movie]

00:03:05.927 --> 00:03:08.989
and Joe Carnahan [co-writer] allow all of
you to improv with dialogue?

00:03:08.989 --> 00:03:11.180
Or were they very strict with sticking to
the script?

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.599
Raúl: I think we kept to the script.

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:16.900
There’s a lot of information in there, a
lot of important, pertinent information and

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.230
I think it was good to stick to it.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:24.000
I mean there were moments when we got to play
and have some fun but the script I think is

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.129
so well written we played within the script
and we were good.

00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:31.370
George: I think adlibbing is only good in
comedies.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:35.790
I don’t think it’s good in dramatic because
it’s not going to make it more sadder or

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:36.790
dramatic.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:37.790
“Wow!

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:39.279
He’s really gone.”

00:03:39.279 --> 00:03:41.920
He’s already gone.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.560
Am I right?

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.319
Raúl: Yes.

00:03:46.319 --> 00:03:51.930
Aimee: The only improv I did was just add
Spanish which Ben was completely cool with.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:56.439
I said “Look they are having this really
intimate conversation and they’ve been together

00:03:56.439 --> 00:04:00.470
forever and I just feel like she would speak
Spanish to him.”

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:01.470
And he was like “Please!”

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:07.151
So there’s a couple things that I said in
that scene that I just spoke Spanish to him

00:04:07.151 --> 00:04:10.542
but other than that yes, it’s so well-written
you don’t have to mess with it.

00:04:10.542 --> 00:04:13.830
George: Was there chemistry between you two
because I don’t see it in the movie but

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:16.670
was it when you guys were filming?

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:17.670
Aimee: Ahhhh….Raúl!

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.290
George: Was there or not?

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:22.650
Aimee: Is there such thing as too much chemistry
George?

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:24.820
George: Was there a thing as too much?

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:27.280
Film Courage: That’s the 6th thing you don’t
do on set.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:33.420
George: Well you guys got along, it was very
sweet your relationship.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:38.790
Raúl: Yes, it was important and Ben and Joe
had us meet well before we even went off to

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:43.070
shoot the movie and I think that added to
the chemistry.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:47.510
I think he gave us a little bit of history
before we walked on set and in that way.

00:04:47.510 --> 00:04:51.170
I think that was important that we spent time
before we landed in Calgary.

00:04:51.170 --> 00:04:56.590
Aimee: When we met Joe for the first time
Joe’s like “You guys have known each other

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:57.650
forever.”

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:00.100
And he said “Ben, how long have they known
each other?”

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:03.450
And we’re like “We’ve just met an hour
ago.”

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:04.500
And Joe couldn’t believe it.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.310
Remember we went out for Tequila?

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:07.540
Raúl: I picked you up.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:11.750
Aimee: And by the time the car ride ended
we just…I don’t know?

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:14.440
I felt like I’d known you for a long time?

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:19.940
Film Courage: Last story…anything that happened
on set where it seemed like it was going to

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:24.420
be a disaster and then at the last minute
something happened and it pulled it all together?

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.250
Aimee: How much time do you have?

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:27.250
No I’m kidding!

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:28.250
I’m kidding.

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:31.740
Raúl: I mean it’s independent filmmaking
so there’s all kinds of little disasters

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:35.610
but it’s how well people deal with them
and that’s kind of all part of filmmaking

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:40.510
at least in television you develop a rhythm
to shooting and an in television it’s over

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:45.060
and and by the time you start developing something
there’s this kind of rhythm but you can

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:51.880
really tell the character of the crew and
how they handle all of those little accidents

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:59.390
and I think the crew and everyone gelled really
well and dealt with adversity in the best

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:01.010
way you could ask for.

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:06.600
George: We have a scene (Raúl and I) in the
ambulance and it’s very tight so Raúl is

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:12.240
laying down and I’m over there and the paramedic
is…I mean he really was acting “You’re

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:13.670
going to be alright!”

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:17.660
He’s getting in front of the camera “Hang
on!”

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:24.040
And Ben is like “Hey that dude, this dude
needs to bring it down a little bit.”

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:28.530
So I think he just ended up not talking and
putting an IV in.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:32.450
Aimee: George that was his dream as an actor.

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:34.210
George: “Just take it easy.”

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:40.060
Hey man this guy is too loud for this little
area.

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:43.360
He’s ordering a nurse around, there’s
no nurse in there.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.360
“I need some saline, stat.”

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:49.479
We’re the only ones in there.

00:06:49.479 --> 00:06:54.060
So I think (there’s another) you can do
too much because it’s the one thing he holds

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.300
it up “I think this is going to work.”

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.940
The thing comes up in the air.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:02.340
Aimee: I will say George being a stand-up
comedian he is unrecognizable in this film.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:11.930
His dramatic work as is piercing and grounded
and connected and I’m so proud…I just

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:13.270
want to say that.

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:14.270
I’m very excited.

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:18.290
George: You got me to read the script five
months…she was telling me how great the

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:19.290
movie was and I’m golfing.

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:26.280
I don’t have time so it was going to go
away and she said really like I’m telling

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:28.200
you, don’t miss this opportunity.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:32.310
And I’m glad that I partially read it.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:34.170
Raúl: You read just your parts?

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:39.180
George: I’m dyslexic man…just give me
an “exactlapedia Britannica” to look at.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:42.850
Exactlapedia…that’s what I call it.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:45.060
Aimee: Thank you.

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:46.120
I know we’re gabbers.

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:47.120
George: Exactlapedia…

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:47.760
Aimee: Thank you.

